messages.content:
To create a standalone application that acts like RAWM without touching your existing PowerShell app, you can build a simple .NET executable that runs a custom PowerShell script.

Here is a complete, minimal C# console application that:
1.  Creates a hidden script file in your Start Menu folder.
2.  Executes that script using PowerShell.
3.  Registers itself as a Start Menu shortcut.

**Assumptions:**
*   You have .NET SDK installed.
*   You want the app to run silently in the background or as a user-initiated shortcut.
*   The script logic (the "RAWM" functionality) will be injected into the generated `.ps1` file.

### `Program.cs`

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Diagnostics;
using System.Linq;
using System.Reflection;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string startMenuPath = Environment.GetFolderPath(Environment.SpecialFolder.StartMenu) + @"\Programs";
        string scriptName = "RawmApp.ps1";
        string scriptPath = Path.Combine(startMenuPath, scriptName);
        string exeName = Assembly.GetEntryAssembly().GetName().Name;
        string exePath = Path.Combine(startMenuPath, exeName);

        // 1. Create the PowerShell script file
        string scriptContent = @"
# RAWM Standalone Script
Write-Host 'RAWM Standalone Executed' -ForegroundColor Cyan
# Insert your actual RAWM logic here
Write-Host 'Logic executed successfully.'
";

        File.WriteAllText(scriptPath, scriptContent);

        // 2. Create the executable shortcut
        string shortcutPath = Path.Combine(startMenuPath, exeName + ".lnk");
        CreateShortcut(shortcutPath, exePath);

        // 3. Run the script immediately (optional, remove if you want it to be a launcher only)
        RunScript(scriptPath);

        Console.WriteLine("Setup complete. Shortcut created at: " + shortcutPath);
        Console.WriteLine("Press any key to exit...");
        Console.ReadKey();
    }

    static void CreateShortcut(string shortcutPath, string targetPath)
    {
        using (var shortcut = new System.Windows.Forms.Shortcut(shortcutPath))
        {
            shortcut.TargetPath = targetPath;
            shortcut.WorkingDirectory = Path.GetDirectoryName(targetPath);
            shortcut.Save();
        }
    }

    static void RunScript(string scriptPath)
    {
        var startInfo = new ProcessStartInfo
        {
            FileName = "powershell.exe",
            Arguments = $"-NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File \"{scriptPath}\"",
            UseShellExecute = true,
            WorkingDirectory = Path.GetDirectoryName(scriptPath)
        };

        Process.Start(startInfo);
    }
}
```

### How to use this:
1.  Save the code as `Program.cs` in a new folder.
2.  Initialize a project: `dotnet new console -n RawmApp`.
3.  Replace the contents of `Program.cs` with the code above.
4.  Run: `dotnet run`.
5.  A shortcut will appear in your Start Menu, and the script will run immediately.

This keeps your original PowerShell app completely untouched while giving you a dedicated, installable entry point for the functionality.
